{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 901 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "9cd2bbc5674bb55c030b18a7d67cb1a09a0c3fe2024104136f34b24e4d0af762" :edb/db-before-t 900 :edb/db-after-t 901 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534213 50 #inst "2026-10-06T14:44:16.910Z" 13194139534213 true] [17592186115102 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282681 13194139534213 true] [17592186115143 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282693 13194139534213 true] [17592186115229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282674 13194139534213 true] [17592186115229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282688 13194139534213 true] [17592186115634 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534213 true] [17592186115634 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\cot x = \\sqrt{3}$ for $- π < x < π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?" 13194139534213 false] [17592186115634 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534213 true] [17592186115634 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add or subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" 13194139534213 true] [17592186282674 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282675 13194139534213 true] [17592186282674 1089 "q-120646" 13194139534213 true] [17592186282674 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534213 true] [17592186282674 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534213 true] [17592186282674 1113 #uuid "1e429c53-db66-50b4-900e-8aac7266bdd4" 13194139534213 true] [17592186282674 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$" 13194139534213 true] [17592186282675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282676 13194139534213 true] [17592186282675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282677 13194139534213 true] [17592186282675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282678 13194139534213 true] [17592186282675 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282679 131